<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minamoto clan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/814">814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Genji, Genke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minamoto was a noble surname that the Emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> gave, from 814, to members of the imperial family excluded from the succession and demoted into the nobility; Emperor Saga first granted it to his seventh son. The Minamoto were one of the four great clans of Heian politics, with the Fujiwara, Taira and Tachibana. Their most notable line, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seiwa-genji">Seiwa Genji</a>, became a warrior dynasty that restored order for the Fujiwara. After defeats in 1156 and 1160, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> rose against the Taira in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> of 1180–1185, destroyed them, and in 1192 became shōgun, founding Japan's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>. The Ashikaga and Tokugawa claimed Minamoto descent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minamoto-clan.jpg" alt="Minamoto clan" width="330" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Utagawa Kuniyoshi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genealogy-of-the-Minamoto-Clan-Utagawa-Kuniyoshi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The name removed its holders from the royal class altogether, a reduction in rank even for princes of the ō rank who were not in the line of succession. There were 21 branches of the clan, each named after the emperor from whom it descended, and some were populous while a few left no descendants. Among the Saga Genji, descended from Emperor Saga, three sons became Minister of the Left and were among the most powerful figures at court in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a>, and some descendants of Minamoto no Tōru settled in the provinces as warrior houses, among them the Watanabe and Matsuura. The Kazan Genji became the Shirakawa family, which headed the Jingi-kan, the office for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seiwa-genji">Seiwa Genji</a> descended from Minamoto no Tsunemoto (897–961), a grandson of Emperor Seiwa, who went to the provinces and founded a major warrior dynasty. Minamoto no Mitsunaka (912–997) allied with the Fujiwara, who afterwards often called on the Minamoto to restore order in Heian-Kyō. His son Yorinobu suppressed the rebellion of Taira no Tadatsune in 1032, and Yorinobu's son Yoriyoshi and grandson Yoshiie pacified most of northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> between 1051 and 1087.</p><p>The Taira executed most of the line, including Minamoto no Tameyoshi, in the Hōgen Rebellion of 1156, and Minamoto no Yoshitomo died in the Heiji Disturbance of 1160. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-no-kiyomori">Taira no Kiyomori</a> then exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> (1147–1199), who in 1180 mounted the rebellion that destroyed the Taira within five years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Three eras of Japanese history trace to Minamoto lines. Yoritomo founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> the Muromachi period, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, who claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seiwa-genji">Seiwa Genji</a> through the Nitta clan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. The Nitta, Takeda, Matsudaira, Shimazu, Sasaki and many other clans also formed from Minamoto lines. Evidence suggested in the late 19th century that Tsunemoto was a grandson of Emperor Yōzei rather than Seiwa is not widely accepted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji</a> takes its title from the surname, which its hero Hikaru Genji received from his father the emperor, and the Tale of the Heike tells of the struggle with the Taira.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minamoto clan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1180 to 1185 the Genpei War between the Taira and <b>Minamoto clans</b> ended with the victory of Minamoto no Yoritomo and the founding of the Kamakura shogunate, later dominated by the Hōjō clan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1180 to 1185 the Taira and <b>Minamoto clans</b> fought the Genpei War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Battle of Uji followed Prince Mochihito and Minamoto no Yorimasa's plan to raise an army to overthrow the Taira clan, and an edict urging the <b>Minamoto clan</b>, temples and shrines to revolt.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-20">20 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
